Apply every match is a bug, not a policy.

242
00:15:43.432 --> 00:15:46.973
It is order-dependent, so two servers can quote two prices.

243
00:15:47.253 --> 00:15:50.176
It stacks everything, and it breaks margin floors.

244
00:15:51.526 --> 00:15:56.902
Priority versus best-for-customer is a ~12% revenue decision in L2.

245
00:15:57.182 --> 00:16:02.921
Neither is wrong, but it must be chosen deliberately and shown to the customer (best offer applied).

246
00:16:03.201 --> 00:16:10.945
Harder combinatorics (bundles that share items, cheapest item free across lines) become small optimisation problems.

247
00:16:11.225 --> 00:16:16.067
Bound them: evaluate a few candidate plans and keep the best, with a time budget.

248
00:16:17.417 --> 00:16:19.252
Quotes versus final prices.

249
00:16:19.532 --> 00:16:21.495
Cart quote: fast and advisory.

250
00:16:21.775 --> 00:16:23.865
It may use cached prices seconds old.

251
00:16:24.145 --> 00:16:27.708
Checkout price: recomputed server-side with the current rules.

252
00:16:27.988 --> 00:16:34.345
If it differs from what the customer last saw, the customer sees the change before paying (Ch 46).

253
00:16:34.625 --> 00:16:38.593
Order snapshot: immutable line-level amounts with rule versions.

254
00:16:38.873 --> 00:16:46.575
Everything after checkout (invoice, GST filing, refunds, seller settlement in Ch 45) reads the snapshot.

255
00:16:46.855 --> 00:16:48.241
