716
00:48:25.318 --> 00:48:27.899
How do you roll out a mass price change for a sale?

717
00:48:28.179 --> 00:48:29.545
Thirty-second answer:

718
00:48:29.825 --> 00:48:43.627
Pre-publish price books with a future effective time; nodes load them ahead and switch at that instant; bounds and approvals on large changes; monitoring of discount depth and orders below cost; kill switch and rollback.

719
00:48:44.977 --> 00:48:45.745
Senior.

720
00:48:46.025 --> 00:48:47.071
Question 10.

721
00:48:47.351 --> 00:48:50.337
How do you stop a campaign budget from running out in hours?

722
00:48:50.617 --> 00:48:51.983
Thirty-second answer:

723
00:48:52.263 --> 00:49:05.553
Pace it: a controller adjusts the probability of showing the offer every few minutes so spend follows the expected traffic curve (with known events included), while the atomic budget counter enforces the hard limit.

724
00:49:06.903 --> 00:49:08.034
Question 11.

725
00:49:08.314 --> 00:49:09.914
How do you know a coupon worked?

726
00:49:10.194 --> 00:49:11.559
Thirty-second answer:

727
00:49:11.839 --> 00:49:20.181
Measure incremental orders against a randomised holdout that never sees it; redemptions alone count people who would have bought anyway.

728
00:49:20.461 --> 00:49:22.530
Then target by predicted uplift.

729
00:49:23.880 --> 00:49:24.968
Question 12.

730
00:49:25.248 --> 00:49:26.848
How would you price perishables?

731
00:49:27.128 --> 00:49:28.493
Thirty-second answer:

732
00:49:28.773 --> 00:49:45.200
Track stock by expiry batch; each day, per SKU, choose a markdown for units near expiry by maximising expected revenue (or margin minus waste cost) given stock, demand and response estimates; bound it; learn response from sell-through.

733
00:49:46.550 --> 00:49:47.830
Question 13.

734
00:49:48.110 --> 00:49:49.923
How do you prevent coupon abuse?

735
00:49:50.203 --> 00:49:51.569
Thirty-second answer:

736
00:49:51.849 --> 00:50:08.254
Per-customer, device and payment-instrument limits; new-account risk signals (device fingerprint, phone age, address reuse); rate-limited code attempts; unguessable codes; release on cancellation only after checks; monitoring of redemption clusters.

737
00:50:09.604 --> 00:50:10.799
Question 14.

738
00:50:11.079 --> 00:50:13.980
How do brand-funded promotions change the design?

739
00:50:14.260 --> 00:50:15.625
Thirty-second answer:

740
00:50:15.905 --> 00:50:28.364
Every line's discount needs a funding source so the brand can be billed or settled; stacking rules may differ by funder; reporting per brand; contracts cap spend; the allocation per line makes it possible.

741
00:50:29.714 --> 00:50:30.610
Architect.

742
00:50:30.890 --> 00:50:32.085
Question 15.

743
00:50:32.365 --> 00:50:36.269
Design pricing and promotions for a large grocery platform.

744
00:50:36.549 --> 00:50:37.914
Thirty-second answer:

745
00:50:38.194 --> 00:51:01.405
Price books with effective dates and MRP; promotion rules as versioned data with groups, floors and budgets; a rules compiler feeding in-memory indexes in stateless quote nodes; deterministic combination; checkout reprice and immutable snapshots with per-line allocations; pacing and uplift targeting for campaigns; markdown optimiser; audit and governance.

746
00:51:02.755 --> 00:51:03.949
Question 16.

747
00:51:04.229 --> 00:51:06.000
How do you test a pricing engine?

748
00:51:06.280 --> 00:51:07.645
Thirty-second answer:

749
00:51:07.925 --> 00:51:26.144
Golden carts with expected totals reviewed by finance; property tests (rule order shuffles give the same result; allocations sum; no line below floor or above MRP); replay of production carts against a new engine version (shadow pricing) with diff reports; canary by store.

750
00:51:27.494 --> 00:51:28.838
Question 17.

751
00:51:29.118 --> 00:51:32.254
What are the regulatory constraints on pricing in India?

752
00:51:32.534 --> 00:51:33.899
Thirty-second answer:

753
00:51:34.179 --> 00:51:51.673
Packaged goods can't be sold above MRP (Legal Metrology rules); GST invoices have format and rounding requirements; e-commerce rules require clear price display and prohibit dark patterns such as drip pricing and false urgency (2023 guidelines).

754
00:51:51.953 --> 00:51:53.787
Verify specifics with legal.

755
00:51:55.137 --> 00:51:56.311
Question 18.

756
00:51:56.591 --> 00:51:58.297
Should prices be personalised?

757
00:51:58.577 --> 00:51:59.943
Thirty-second answer:

758
00:52:00.223 --> 00:52:10.591
Personalised offers (coupons for segments) are common and defensible with clear rules; personalised base prices risk trust, fairness and regulatory problems.

759
00:52:10.871 --> 00:52:17.399
If done, use non-sensitive attributes, cap differences, audit for disparate impact, and be transparent.

760
00:52:18.749 --> 00:52:19.986
Question 19.

761
00:52:20.266 --> 00:52:24.426
How do pricing and promotions interact with search and recommendations?

762
00:52:24.706 --> 00:52:26.071
Thirty-second answer:

763
00:52:26.351 --> 00:52:42.138
Search and feeds show prices and offer badges from cached price or promotion indexes; they must match checkout within tolerance; events keep them fresh; recommendations may use margin and promotions but must respect sponsored-content disclosure.

764
00:52:43.488 --> 00:52:44.597
Question 20.

765
00:52:44.877 --> 00:52:47.480
What metrics prove the pricing platform is healthy?

766
00:52:47.760 --> 00:52:49.125
Thirty-second answer:

767
00:52:49.405 --> 00:53:06.728
Quote latency and version consistency; paise reconciliation errors (zero); lines below floor or above MRP (zero); discount spend versus plan; incremental orders per campaign versus holdout; waste and markdown sell-through; mistaken-price incidents.

768
00:53:08.078 --> 00:53:08.974
Part 9.

769
00:53:09.254 --> 00:53:10.662
Summary and Practice.

770
00:53:11.912 --> 00:53:13.000
Key takeaways.

771
00:53:13.280 --> 00:53:18.016
Integer paise, largest-remainder allocation, and a stored snapshot (L1).

772
00:53:18.296 --> 00:53:24.909
Indexed rules with explicit combination groups, floors and a deliberate choice of which offer wins (L2).

773
00:53:25.189 --> 00:53:29.413
Pace budgets and target incrementality, measured with holdouts (L3).

774
00:53:29.693 --> 00:53:34.963
Markdowns sized per SKU recover margin from expiring stock (L4).

775
00:53:35.243 --> 00:53:39.445
Govern price changes: bounds, approvals, MRP, disclosure.

776
00:53:40.795 --> 00:53:42.118
Decision cheat sheet.

777
00:53:42.398 --> 00:53:43.763
Here is a quick table:

778
00:53:44.043 --> 00:53:45.217
Need, and Choice.

779
00:53:45.497 --> 00:53:48.910
Money: long paise; HALFUP per line; discount before tax.

780
00:53:49.190 --> 00:53:50.491
Cart-level discounts:

781
00:53:50.771 --> 00:53:52.947
Largest remainder, stored per line.

782
00:53:53.227 --> 00:53:54.209
Offer lookup:

783
00:53:54.489 --> 00:53:56.451
Inverted indexes, hot-swapped.

784
00:53:56.731 --> 00:53:57.734
Combination:

785
00:53:58.014 --> 00:54:01.961
ITEM, then CART, then PAYMENT, then SHIPPING, one per group, floors.

786
00:54:02.241 --> 00:54:03.115
Which offer:

787
00:54:03.395 --> 00:54:07.641
Best-for-customer or priority, decided by Finance and documented.

788
00:54:07.921 --> 00:54:08.774
Budgets:

789
00:54:09.054 --> 00:54:11.422
Atomic counter + pacing controller.

790
00:54:11.702 --> 00:54:12.705
Effectiveness:

791
00:54:12.985 --> 00:54:14.777
Holdouts, uplift targeting.

792
00:54:15.057 --> 00:54:16.038
Perishables:

793
00:54:16.318 --> 00:54:19.305
Per-SKU expected-revenue markdowns, bounded.

794
00:54:20.655 --> 00:54:21.785
Exercises.

795
00:54:22.065 --> 00:54:29.809
(B2) In L1, implement allocation by eligible lines only (a coupon for fruit) and prove the sums still hold.

796
00:54:30.089 --> 00:54:36.084
(B3) In L2, add a buy any 3 from category, cheapest free offer and bound its plan search.

797
00:54:36.364 --> 00:54:41.655
(B3) In L3, add the TV ad to the expected curve and re-run the pacing.

798
00:54:41.935 --> 00:54:49.615
(B4) In L4, add a waste cost of 10 rupeesper unit to K4's objective and see how the policy changes.

799
00:54:49.895 --> 00:54:54.631
(B4) Design the promotion rules compiler, including versioning and rollback.

